      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Ibuprofen 1600 mg four times daily comes to 6,400 mg/day, which is twice the usual maximum for adults.\n\n- **Daily dose:** The maximum is 3,200 mg/day for prescription use in a hospital. For OTC use it is 1,200 mg/day.\n- **Single dose:** 1,600 mg in one dose is double the usual 800 mg maximum.\n- **Risks at this dose:** It raises the risk of GI bleeding, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular events. Normal kidney and liver function and no allergies don't change that. Higher doses add toxicity but no extra analgesic or anti-inflammatory benefit.\n\nHere are some options that fit within standard dosing:\n- **Ibuprofen 800 mg PO, 4 times daily:** 3,200 mg/day, the maximum. This is sometimes used for inflammatory conditions.\n- **Ibuprofen 400\u2013600 mg PO, 4 times daily:** 1,600\u20132,400 mg/day. This is typical for pain or fever.\n- **Ibuprofen 1,600 mg/day total:** This may be what was meant, for example 400 mg 4 times daily. It could have been a total-daily-dose versus per-dose mix-up.\n\nTell me which dose and frequency you want and I'll enter it. If you really do intend 1,600 mg per dose, please tell me the clinical rationale. I would still suggest checking with pharmacy first."
      }
     ],
